freeCAD: 3D CAD with Motion Simulation  by  askoh.com
Assembly
	Notes
		(Text string: 'CAD: Copyright (C) 2000-2004, Aik-Siong Koh, All Rights Reserved.<n><n>An upright cylinder is connected to ground with a translational (slider) joint which allows motion in the y direction. Gravity acts in the negative y direction. A spring-damper connects the cylinder to a ground attachment directly overhead. The cylinder is allowed to oscillate under gravity. A second upright cylinder slides vertically and is offset from the first. It too has a spring-damper on top. A third upright cylinder slides in the same axis as the second. It is connected to the bottom of the second cylinder by another spring-damper. The double mass-spring-damper system is allowed to oscillate in gravity.<n><n>If the instructions below are too brief, refer to the Notes in projectile.asm and circular.asm.<n><n>To load the example for a quick look:<n>Click File/Open/Assembly/ to get a dialog. Enter *.asm for a list of assemblies. Select springs.asm. <n><n>To create the assembly from scratch:<n>To create three cylinders:<n>Create an empty assembly and populate it with three cylinders (Assembly1Part1, Assembly1Part2, Assembly1Part3). The first and second each has radius (0.3d) height (1.0d). The third has radius (0.2d) height (0.5d). Set the density of each cylinder to (10.0d). <n><n>To mark the joint attachment points:<n>Create a marker (Assembly1Marker1) on the assembly at (0.0d, 3.0d, 0.0d). z-axis point (0.0d, 1.0d, 0.0d). x-axis point (1.0d, 0.0d, 0.0d).<n>Create a second marker (Assembly1Marker2) on the assembly at (1.0d, 3.0d, 0.0d). z-axis point (0.0d, 1.0d, 0.0d). x-axis point (1.0d, 0.0d, 0.0d).<n>On the first cylinder, create a marker (Assembly1Part1Marker1) at (0.0d, 0.0d, 0.0d) relative to the z-face. <n>On the second cylinder, create a marker (Assembly1Part2Marker1) at (0.0d, 0.0d, 0.0d) and another (Assembly1Part2Marker2) at (0.0d, 0.0d, -1.0d) relative to the z-face. <n>On the third cylinder, create a marker (Assembly1Part3Marker1) at (0.0d, 0.0d, 0.0d) relative to the z-face.<n><n>To create the translational joints:<n>A translational (slider) joint constraints the z axes of ''i'' and ''j'' to be colinear, the x axes to be parallel and the y axes to be parallel. Only one translational degree of freedom exists between ''i'' and ''j''.<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker1) to (Assembly1Part1Marker1) with translational joint (Assembly1Joint1).<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker2) to (Assembly1Part2Marker1) with translational joint (Assembly1Joint2).<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker2) to (Assembly1Part3Marker1) with translational joint (Assembly1Joint3).<n>Tilt each cylinder to make the z-face point up. This will help the solver satisfy the translational joints more easily.<n><n>To connect the cylinders with spring-damper combinations:<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker1) to (Assembly1Part1Marker1) with a Dynamic/ForceTorque/InLine (Assembly1Force1).<n>Click radio button ''tension''. Click Example/SpringDamper/Linear to get the equation<n>F0 + K*(rIJ(self) - L0) + C*vrIJ(self)<n>where F0 = set force, K = stiffness, rIJ() = distance from I to J, L0 = set length, C = damping coeff., vrIJ() = radial velocity from I to J, self = the ForceTorque containing the equation.<n>Subsititute F0 = 0, K = 100, L0 = 1, C = 0 to get the formula<n>0 + 100*(rIJ(self) - 1) + 0*vrIJ(self)<n>Click the radio button ''tension'' again to accept the equation. The ''twistIJ'' equation is not needed and can be left empty. Click OK.<n>There is no harm leaving those zero terms in the equation for clarity. The symbolic parser simplifies all equations for internal use.<n>Note that the Force direction is ill defined when rIJ() is near zero.<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker2) to (Assembly1Part2Marker1) with Dynamic/ForceTorque/InLine (Assembly1Force2).<n>Enter tension<n>0 + 150*(rIJ(self) - 1) + 0*vrIJ(self)<n>Connect (Assembly1Marker2) to (Assembly1Part3Marker1) with Dynamic/ForceTorque/InLine (Assembly1Force3).<n>Enter tension<n>0 + 50*(rIJ(self) - 1) + 0*vrIJ(self)<n><n>To set the initial conditions:<n>It is possible to set the initial positions and initial velocities of the cylinders precisely by RightClick/PositionPart/Direction/ and RightClick/Velocity/ over any part marker.<n><n>To set gravity:<n>Click Dynamic/Gravity/ for the gravity dialog. Enter (0.0d, -9.81d, 0.0d).<n><n>The assembly is now ready for simulation, animation and plotting.<n>You can run a Quasi Static simulation to find the static equillibrium configuration of the system.<n>' runs: (Core.RunArray runs: #(686 63 14 5 12 1 2 37 89 11 4 36 1 26 262 36 678 35 627 57 1175 30 181 15 242) values: #(nil #underline #(#underline #bold) #underline #(#underline #bold) #underline nil #(#bold #large) nil #bold nil #(#bold #large) nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil #bold nil)))
